WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.917
Picture the bank's app on a phone.

2
00:00:04.917 --> 00:00:11.790
A customer taps send, and a request, one message asking the bank to move money, reaches the bank's program.

3
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.295
Thousands of customers do this at once.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:17.458
Before money moves, the bank asks two questions.

5
00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:21.759
An outside safety service says whether the transfer looks like theft.

6
00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:24.127
It usually answers in 20 milliseconds.

7
00:00:24.127 --> 00:00:26.184
On a bad day, it takes 2 seconds.

8
00:00:26.184 --> 00:00:29.924
And a quick check asks if the transfer fits the daily limit.

9
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.215
So a request spends almost all its time waiting.

10
00:00:34.215 --> 00:00:41.973
At 2,000 requests a second, and 150 milliseconds each, 300 are in flight: in progress at the same time.

11
00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:45.212
On the safety service's bad day, 4,000 are.

12
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:53.218
One platform thread per request would be the restaurant's personal waiter, who stands at the kitchen door for the whole meal.

13
00:00:53.218 --> 00:00:57.647
Each one is an operating system thread, with about a megabyte for its stack.

14
00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:00.503
Four thousand of them, hired at the worst moment.

15
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:07.758
So we take the newer restaurant: a few waiters, and an order slip for each table, clipped to a board while it waits.

16
00:01:07.758 --> 00:01:09.592
A virtual thread is that slip.

17
00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:13.994
While it waits, it lets go of its carrier, the real thread that runs it.

18
00:01:13.994 --> 00:01:17.845
Four thousand waiting requests are four thousand small objects.

19
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:21.644
Which brings up a question we're a little shy to ask.

20
00:01:21.644 --> 00:01:24.989
If threads are this cheap, why worry about limits at all?

21
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:28.052
Here's what the old pool was quietly doing.

22
00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:33.820
A pool of, say, 200 threads let at most 200 requests call the safety service at once.

23
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.788
Nobody wrote that limit down.

24
00:01:35.788 --> 00:01:37.824
It came free with the threads.

25
00:01:37.824 --> 00:01:44.068
Take the pool away, and on the bad day all 4,000 call at once, and each one waits 2 seconds.

26
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:48.304
The safety service's bad day just became everyone's bad day.

27
00:01:48.304 --> 00:01:52.408
So the point is: threads are free, but what they call is not.

28
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:57.065
So every scarce thing gets a limit, written down where we can see it.

29
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:04.984
First, the safety service gets the grill tokens: one shared semaphore, a counter of permits, with 20 on the hook.

30
00:02:04.984 --> 00:02:08.413
A request holds a token only during its safety call.

31
00:02:08.413 --> 00:02:17.116
A limit like this, on one slow outside service, is a bulkhead, named after the walls that keep one flooded room from sinking a ship.

32
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:24.728
Second, the journal: last sitting's 8 cooks and their rail of 80 slots, with the doorman who says busy when it's full.

33
00:02:24.728 --> 00:02:26.436
It stays a pool on purpose.

34
00:02:26.436 --> 00:02:28.396
It isn't there to save threads.

35
00:02:28.396 --> 00:02:30.925
It's a limit, with a rule for saying no.

36
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:36.531
Third, each request gets one deadline: 300 milliseconds for all of its checks.

37
00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:40.595
Every wait inside the request uses whatever is left of it.

38
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:42.697
Now, Little's law at the hook.

39
00:02:42.697 --> 00:02:45.643
Twenty tokens, each held for 20 milliseconds.

40
00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:48.327
How many safety checks a second can pass?

41
00:02:51.333 --> 00:02:55.891
Twenty divided by 0.02 seconds: 1,000 a second.

42
00:02:55.891 --> 00:02:59.091
Plenty for 400 requests a second.

43
00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:05.392
On the bad day, each token is held until the request's deadline, 300 milliseconds.

44
00:03:05.392 --> 00:03:06.363
How many now?

45
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.337
About 67 a second.

46
00:03:10.337 --> 00:03:14.795
The rest wait for a token until their own deadline runs out.

47
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:20.148
And here's the trap from the kitchen timer: a timeout ends the wait, not the work.

48
00:03:20.148 --> 00:03:23.963
An abandoned safety call keeps running, and keeps its token.

49
00:03:23.963 --> 00:03:28.415
On the bad day, abandoned calls hold all 20, and nobody else gets one.

50
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.650
So at the deadline, the request interrupts its own call: a polite request to stop.

51
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:39.131
The call's finally block hangs the token back on the hook, and the next request takes it.

52
00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.470
Then a plain business rule decides.

53
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.689
Small transfers, up to 50.00, go through without the safety answer.

54
00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:47.766
Larger ones get a fast try again.

55
00:03:47.766 --> 00:03:51.859
Where to draw that line is a decision about risk, not about code.

56
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.901
The lesson's demo sends 400 requests a second for 3 seconds.

57
00:03:56.901 --> 00:04:00.168
On a normal day, all 1,200 are approved.

58
00:04:00.168 --> 00:04:06.539
On the bad day, 312 small ones are let through, and 888 are told to try again.

59
00:04:06.539 --> 00:04:14.299
The safety service never sees more than 20 calls, and the slowest reply takes 326 milliseconds.

60
00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:20.447
The safety service had a terrible afternoon, and no customer waited more than a third of a second.

61
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.086
One wait has no deadline at all: the takeaway ticket from the ledger pool.

62
00:04:25.086 --> 00:04:31.451
Once the pool accepts a transfer, the money may move, and a timeout would end the wait, not the transfer.

63
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:34.952
Here's the bulkhead in Java and Kotlin.

64
00:04:34.952 --> 00:04:38.847
Wait for a token, but only as long as the deadline allows.

65
00:04:38.847 --> 00:04:40.929
Hold it only for the slow call.

66
00:04:40.929 --> 00:04:43.816
And hand it back in finally, on every path.

67
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:48.143
Two more rules finish the front door, later in this lesson.

68
00:04:48.143 --> 00:04:52.046
The first no wins, like the head waiter calling his helpers back.

69
00:04:52.046 --> 00:04:55.649
And nothing slow happens under an account lock: the hot pan.

70
00:04:56.333 --> 00:04:58.584
So, if threads are free, why limits?

71
00:04:58.584 --> 00:05:01.273
Because the limit was never on the threads.

72
00:05:01.273 --> 00:05:02.711
It's on what they call.

73
00:05:02.711 --> 00:05:07.901
Give every request its own virtual thread, and write a limit on every scarce thing.

74
00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:08.868
Quick check.

75
00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:11.203
Drop the interrupt at the deadline.

76
00:05:11.203 --> 00:05:14.273
On the bad day, what happens to the 20 tokens?

77
00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:23.652
Abandoned calls keep them for the full 2 seconds, so new requests get none, even for a call that would succeed.
